<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chap-signed-measures" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Signed Measures</title>

<introduction>
<blockquote>
<p><em><q>It is astonishing that the human intellect should ever have tolerated such an absurdity as the idea of a quantity less than nothing.</q></em></p>
<attribution>
Augustus De Morgan
</attribution>
</blockquote>

<p>We have defined measures to only take non-negative values. However, there are some points where it is beneficial to extend the definition of a measure to include negative values (and we will see more as we proceed). For example, <xref ref="exer-measures-vec-space"/> shows that the collection of measures on <m>(X,\mathcal{F})</m> is closed under addition and multiplying by non-negative scalars. The set of measures on <m>(X,\mathcal{F})</m> is <em>almost</em> a vector space; we just can't multiply be negative scalars. Allowing for measures which take positive and negative values would allow us to create a vector space.
This vector space will play a very important role later, see  <xref ref="sec-RMK" /></p>

<p>Less abstractly, and more immediately, in <xref ref="sec-borel-on-R"/>, we showed that if <m>\mu</m> is a Borel measure on <m>[a,b]</m> then the cumulative distribution function for <m>\mu</m>, <m>g_\mu</m>, is an increasing, right-continuous function, <xref ref="thm-cdf"/>. Conversely, given an increasing, right-continuous function <m>g</m> on <m>[a,b]</m>, <m>g</m> determines a Borel measure on <m>[a,b]</m>, <xref ref="thm-lebesgue-stieltjes-measure"/>. Cumulative distribution functions are increasing precisely because measures take non-negative values. In <xref ref="thm-lebesgue-stieltjes-measure"/>, we need an increasing function in order to define a measure. If we want to use measure theory to study real functions, other than increasing functions, we will need to allow for measures which allow for possibly negative values.</p>

<p>
    In <xref ref="sec-signed-measure"/> we give the definition of a signed measure, and prove several general results about signed measures. In <xref ref="sec-signed-borel"/> we apply the results from <xref ref="sec-signed-measure"/> and <xref ref="sec-borel-on-R"/> to study Borel signed measures on <m>\bR</m> and functions of bounded variation.
</p>

</introduction>

